f course i think when you provide  information you need to explain it  uh it doesn't really make any sense to  just give numbers without  explaining what the implications are but  i'm not really in favor of suppressing  information no  the health secretary this morning is  saying that we need to learn to accept  covid and treat it essentially as  the same as flu well each year  according to the ons we uh  see something like 20 22 000 deaths  from flu um do you  accept that that actually that is what  we're going to have to live with now  it's inevitable  uh it looks very likely that this  coronavirus like the others before it  will be with us  for the long term um i think in the  short term  exactly what it does and how much  trouble it causes around the world  looks pretty daunting uh in the longer  term i think it it remains with us  and just how benign it becomes over time  is a matter of speculation  uh but but yes i don't think we're going  to see this virus disappear i think it's  going to  join all the others that cause trouble  for us every year  and we are going to have to learn to  live with it in the longer term yes  so more excess deaths would um the  i know you can't preempt jcvi decisions  but um do you expect therefore for  people  to uh receive a booster vaccination  to add to the regular flu vaccination  each year  uh well certainly we've made  recommendations in the last week to  uh if you like provide us a sense of  direction as to how a program like that  would look  and who would be targeted for boosters  if it proves necessary  i don't think it's absolutely uh 100  sure that we need to do that but i think  we need to be prepared for the  possibility  uh particularly if we begin to see signs  of waning  vaccine protection or escape from the  protection that we've got from the  vaccines as the back as the virus  evolves  so we need to be ready to to do a  booster program if it if we need to  so you are worried about a fourth wave  or not  worried about a fourth wave okay you  mean one after this one  uh i i think i'm most worried really  about  the the global situation uh there are  obviously countries where they're in  fourth and fifth waves now  um and i'm worried about that not just  out of a sense  of um fairness for the people around the  world but actually also for us in the uk  we've had this experience in the last  two months  of importation of a much more infectious  virus  from india that will happen again if the  pandemic goes forward unchecked around  the world and will be left in  in a worse problem and i think that's  the most likely scenario to cause a  fourth or fifth wave  in this country that would be out of  control  adam finn thank you very much indeed  thank you  so jeffrey john donaldson was officially  confirmed as the democratic unionist  party's latest leader this week  it's third in just two months in his  first speech as leader  jeffrey insisted the only path to stable  and sustainable government  includes the removal of the irish sea  border  and earth unity against the brexit deals  northern ireland protocol  which effectively imposed it but the  unity he seeks seems  some way off with the resignation of a  dup storm and assembly member  onto jeffrey's first day in charge  well in his first television interview  since becoming leader sir jeffrey  donaldson spoke to our senior island  correspondent  david blevins about how to tread the  tricky path ahead  and his concerns about rising tension on  the streets of northern ireland  sir jeffrey you have said the removal of  the protocol is the only path to stable  government in northern ireland  what if it isn't removed well at the  heart of the  belfast or good friday agreement um are  three sets of relationships and there's  a very delicate balance within that  agreement as to how  those relationships are managed and one  of the key relationships is that between  northern ireland  and great britain and the agreement is  very clear 