e are  opportunities going forward  but we can't get to those opportunities  because of these unnecessary barriers  much of our supply chain comes from  great britain  whether you are a consumer buying goods  in a supermarket  or a business relying on component parts  for  your manufacturing process we need to  fix that supply chain problem  we need to restore northern ireland  place within the uk  both the market and constitutionally and  if we can do that  then yes we will see the opportunities  that will flow  uh provided we can find practical  solutions on  trade with the eu let's talk for a  moment about  legacy we've seen another two historical  prosecutions  collapse this week you're a former  soldier so you will understand the  concerns of veterans  you're also a victim two of your cousins  were murdered during the troubles  how does northern ireland draw a line  under its difficult past is there an  alternative  to the government's proposal of a  prosecutorial amnesty  uh i think we need a a process that  moves us from where we are  to where we can finally say well look  we've examined the past  we have given people the opportunity and  to  explore whether there is the prospect of  prosecutions  against those who murdered their loved  ones um and  and then yes we need to move to the next  phase of the peace process we need to  move our society  beyond an examination of the past to the  kind of reconciliation and healing  that we desire to see in northern  ireland so  we need some form of process that  recognizes the suffering  of many the injustice that many feel in  northern ireland i don't think you can  pull a veil over that i don't think you  can ignore that  i think that if we're going to have  healing we need to have recognition and  acknowledgement  you are the dups third leader in the  space of two months  is the party imploding because of what  some would consider to be an  own goal on brexit that resulted in a  protocol  and unsettled unionism about  power sharing and the prospects for  irish unity  i don't think it is to do with brexit um  i think that  the issues that have arisen within the  dep  have multiple causes my job now my task  is to bring the party together again  and to together on our common ground  the dup believes passionately in the  union of great britain and northern  ireland we believe  passionately that northern ireland and  all of its people are best served  being part of that united kingdom we  need to broaden the appeal of unionism  and therefore we need to resolve our  internal difficulties i believe we're  well on our way now to doing that the  meeting of the party executive  um this week um saw unanimous support  for  my leadership uh it saw people  coming together acknowledging the hurt  that had been caused on both sides  of the argument and i think now we're  moving away from that  and and we've walked back from the idea  of factionalism  your deputy leader paula bradley this  week apologized  to the lgbtq plus community for what she  described as atrocious  and hurtful comments made by some  elected representatives from the party  in the past  do you share her regret about that and  how will you address any repeat of those  comments  well i think it is true and it's not  just in the case of the dup that things  have been said in the past that have  been hurtful to  people and i think it's right when that  happens that  we say sorry and acknowledge that hurt  equally i hope in time others will be  able to acknowledge that they have  caused hurt  uh for example to people from a strongly  held faith perspective  david the kind of northern ireland i  want is one where we can have respect  for difference  um and in terms of looking ahead i hope  uh that my colleagues will recognize  that when we  take decisions about social issues uh  when we speak out  on social issues which we are entitled  to do that we do so in a gracious way  that we avoid language language that is  seen as intemperate  um or disrespectful but where that  happens i will not hesitate to  ensure that it doesn't happen again  thank you very much for your time  sir jeffrey donaldson with david blevins  there and now  one of the political world's most  well-known oracles  the american pollster and communications  strategist frank luntz is best known for  his analysis of american politics  he hit the headlines earlier this year  when he declared that he'd had enough of  donald trump and the republican party  but he's always retained a keen interest  in uk politics too  ever since advising boris johnson on his  campaign for the presidency of the  oxford union  35 years ago now lunz has undertaken the  survey of this nation for the center for  policy studies  focusing on language and attitudes  towards political  and economic issues and is here to  discuss those findings ahead of the  survey's  full publication later this week  franklin  what were you actually trying to  understand with this survey  well in three words populism locism  and extremism i've seen the damage  that's done to american politics to the  american economy  and i want to see that happen here in  the uk i spent three years at oxford in  the 1980s  i was recently a lecturer for a week at  the vodmic institute at oxford  and i loved it i really do love it here  and i appreciate the civility and the  decency that people  exhibit i appreciate the respect that  they have for different points of view  and it's in essence a warning a wake-up  call  and and a road map for how to avoid the  mess  that we've got in america right now and  i say this on the fourth of july and it  really disappoints me  because i still love my country and i'm  still  i'm still patriotic i still get  emotional when i see the flag  but i get emotional when i see your flag  as well the union jack  and i want to see understanding i want  to see respect i want to see civility  across the political spectrum  so that's why i'm here and that's the  purpose of the cps study  well we were talking earlier on the  program about that issue and we just had  a by-election i think you'll know here  where uh i have to say it wasn't  particularly  um characterized by civility but let's  have a look at the whole picture  let's start with um what people think  about um  political leaders in britain you ask a  question about how political leaders  make people feel  and look at that that's the number one  answer we gave them a list of 18  attributes and disappointed came in  first ignored irrelevant  the first eight words we gave them ten  negative words and eight positive words  and the first eight that they chose were  all negative  that this is a wake-up call for the  conservatives for lib dems for labor for  the greens it's a wake-up call for  everyone in politics  that the public isn't hearing you that  you may be  speaking you may be doing events you may  be sending them emails and text you may  have a website  but to them it's all noise you need to  cut through the clutter and i'm going to  give you three  recommendations for it number one is  that they want to know what you are for  not what you are against so don't define  yourself by the other party define  yourself  by yours number two the highest priority  right now is honesty and trust so be  candid with them  say what you mean and mean what you say  and number three  safety security strong uh successful  that the number one focus is still the  economy  but number two right underneath it and  right behind it is crime  so give people a better quality of life  and they'll give you  a re-election that's um  well that's that's quite a big steer for  us as a programmer it sounds like  we'd um do better in ratings if we had  fewer politicians on bit of a problem  for us but  the the point you make there about  division which we again we discussed  earlier on  um in the united states we see a lot of  division on the  issues of race and so on uh is that  reflected h